# EF Core / SQLite conventions

- Use `IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>` (`AddDbContextFactory` in
  `ServiceCollectionExtensions.AddInfrastructure`), not an injected
  long-lived `AppDbContext`. Every repository method should create its own
  short-lived context via `await using var db = await dbContextFactory.CreateDbContextAsync(ct);`.
  This is the documented pattern for apps without a per-operation DI scope
  (see EF Core docs on `DbContext` lifetime); don't "simplify" it back to a
  singleton `DbContext`.
- Entity configuration goes in an `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` class
  (`ArtistConfiguration`), applied via
  `modelBuilder.ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(...)` — don't inline
  Fluent API in `OnModelCreating`.
- **SQLite can't translate `ORDER BY`/comparisons over `DateTimeOffset`**
  columns (`NotSupportedException` at query time). Store timestamps as UTC
  ticks (`long`) via `HasConversion(v => v.UtcTicks, v => new DateTimeOffset(v, TimeSpan.Zero))`
  and keep the CLR property as `DateTimeOffset` in the domain model — see
  `ArtistConfiguration.cs`. Apply the same conversion to any new
  `DateTimeOffset`/`DateTimeOffset?` column.
- Repository interfaces live in `Application/Abstractions`; implementations
  here must not leak EF Core types (`DbSet<T>`, `IQueryable<T>`,
  `EntityEntry`, ...) through their public surface.
- Migrations: run from this project directory —
  `cd src/PickMeWhatToListen.Infrastructure && dotnet ef migrations add <Name> --output-dir Migrations`.
  `AppDbContextFactory` (`IDesignTimeDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>`) exists
  so this works without booting the WPF host — don't delete it.
- Apply migrations with `Database.MigrateAsync()` (via
  `DatabaseMigrator.MigrateAsync`), never `EnsureCreated()` — the whole
  point is that future schema changes (discography, tags, albums) are
  additive migrations against real user data, not a re-seed.
- After changing the model, regenerate `docs/generated/db-schema.md` to
  match the new migration.
